The autonomous coding agent will be developed only after this foundation has been validated.

4. Initial Product Goal

The first meaningful version of Repo AI should answer:

"Given a new project requirement, which of my previous repositories and components are useful, and how should I reuse them?"

The system should not initially attempt to autonomously build the entire project.

The first objective is intelligence and recommendation, not autonomous code generation.

5. Reuse Philosophy

Repo AI should optimize for:

Maximum useful reuse, not maximum reuse.

A component should not be recommended simply because it exists.

The system should determine whether the component is actually suitable for the new project.

Potential reuse classifications include:

DIRECT_REUSE
ADAPTABLE
REIMPLEMENT
INCOMPATIBLE
HUMAN_REVIEW
6. Development Philosophy

The project will follow these principles:

Deterministic First

Use deterministic program analysis wherever possible.

Examples:

File discovery
AST parsing
Import/export extraction
Dependency analysis
Route detection
Schema detection
Framework detection

AI should be introduced where semantic understanding is genuinely required.

Local-First

The initial system should minimize unnecessary infrastructure and API costs.

Development should prioritize:

Local execution
PostgreSQL
pgvector
Tree-sitter
Local or inexpensive models
Provider abstraction
Modular Monolith First

Repo AI will initially be developed as a modular monolith.

Microservices, queues, and distributed infrastructure should only be introduced when actual requirements justify them.

Validate Before Automating

The system should first prove that its repository intelligence and Blueprint actually save development time.

Autonomous code modification should come later.

7. What We Are NOT Building Yet

At the beginning of development, we deliberately exclude:

Frontend
Autonomous coding agent
Microservices
Distributed queues
Cloud infrastructure
Full SaaS platform
Automatic deployment

These may become relevant later, but they are not part of the initial engineering problem.

8. First Engineering Milestone

The first milestone is:

Convert source code into a structured syntax representation using Tree-sitter.

The first experiment is intentionally tiny:

TypeScript source code
        ↓
Tree-sitter
        ↓
TypeScript grammar
        ↓
Syntax Tree

This establishes the foundation for everything that follows.

